  <section>
    <h4 style="border-left: 3px solid var(--info); padding-left: 8px; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600;">Deep Conceptual Explanation</h4>
    <p>Everyday Chemistry focuses on the application of chemical principles in daily life, specifically focusing on <b>Fertilisers</b> and <b>Fuels</b>. Fertilisers are chemical substances containing essential nutrients like <b>Nitrogen (N)</b>, <b>Phosphorus (P)</b>, and <b>Potassium (K)</b>, known as <b>NPK</b>. These are categorized into organic manures and inorganic chemical fertilisers. The [[Haber Process]] is the industrial cornerstone for synthesizing ammonia, which serves as the precursor for most nitrogenous fertilisers, fundamentally altering global food security by overcoming the limitations of natural soil nitrogen fixation.</p><p>Fuels are substances that release energy upon combustion. They are classified into <b>Primary Fuels</b> (coal, petroleum, natural gas) and <b>Secondary Fuels</b> (coke, gasoline, LPG). The efficiency of a fuel is measured by its <b>Calorific Value</b>. Key mechanisms include the <b>Fractional Distillation</b> of crude oil, where components are separated based on boiling points. In the context of environmental chemistry, the transition from carbon-heavy fuels to <b>Green Hydrogen</b> and <b>Biofuels</b> is critical to reducing the <b>Greenhouse Effect</b> and combating global warming.</p><p>Understanding these concepts is vital for defence aspirants as they intersect with <b>Environmental Science</b> and <b>Strategic Resources</b>. The shift toward sustainable energy and the chemistry of explosives (often derived from nitrate fertilisers) are recurring themes in NDA/CDS exams. Mastery of these topics ensures a comprehensive grasp of how chemical energy powers modern warfare and sustains the civilian population.</p>
  </section>

